Yes, it's possible to implement this behavior using the JIRA ScriptRunner plugin. You can create a custom validation rule that checks whether all items in a specific checklist are marked as done before allowing the issue to be moved to another column. The rule will return an error message if any item is still pending, preventing the issue from being transitioned.

Here's an example of how you could write such a validation rule:

```groovy
import com.onemonks.scriptrunner.validation.*
import com.atlassian.jira.component.*
import com.atlassian.jira.issue.*
import com.atlassian.jira.project.*
import com.atlassian.jira.transition.*
import com.atlassian.jira.workitem.*
import com.atlassian.jira.workitem.manager.*
import com.atlassian.jira.workitem.renderer.*
import com.atlassian.plugins.*
import com.google.common.collect.*

class ChecklistCompleteValidation implements ValidationRule {
    @Inject
    private WorkItemManager wim

    @Inject
    private ComponentAccessor componentAccessor

    @Inject
    private PluginDescriptor descriptor

    private String checklistId = "10000" // replace with your actual checklist ID

    @Override
    public boolean validate(Issue issue, Transition transition) throws Exception {
        boolean valid = true

        List<WorkItem> workItems = wim.getWorkItems(issue, null)
        List<CheckItem> checkItems = Lists.newArrayList()
        for (WorkItem workItem : workItems) {
            if (workItem instanceof CheckListItem && ((CheckListItem) workItem).getCheckList().getId() == Long.parseLong(checklistId)) {
                checkItems.addAll(((CheckListItem) workItem).getCheckItems())
            }
        }
        boolean allDone = checkItems.stream().allMatch { it.isDone() }
        if (!allDone) {
            valid = false
            message = "Please complete all items in checklist ${checklistId} before moving the issue."
        }
        return valid
    }

