In NestJS, you can create a model that creates a MongoDB document with multiple schemas by following these steps:

1. Define your models using Mongoose schema definitions. For example:

```typescript
import { Schema, model } from 'mongoose';

interface UserInfo {
  firstName: string;
  lastName: string;
}

interface Address {
  street: string;
  city: string;
  state: string;
  zip: number;
}

interface ContactInfo {
  email: string;
  phone: string;
}

const userSchema = new Schema({
  username: String,
  password: String,
  info: UserInfo,
  address: Address,
  contacts: [ContactInfo],
});

export const User = model('User', userSchema);
```

2. Use the `model()` method provided by Mongoose to create your model. The `model()` method takes the name of the model (in this case, "User"), the schema definition, and any options you want to pass to Mongoose. In our example, we're not passing any options, so it will use the default ones.

3. Now you can use your model in your NestJS application. Here's an example of how to create a new user:

```typescript
import { Injectable } from '@nestjs/common';
import { User } from './user.model';
import { Model } from 'mongoose';
import { InjectModel } from 'nestjs-typegoose';

@Injectable()
export class UserService {
  constructor(
    @InjectModel(User) private readonly userModel: Model<User>,
  ) {}

  async createUser(userData: any): Promise<User> {
    const newUser = new this.userModel(userData);
    return await newUser.save();
  }
}
```

