To fix the CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing) error in Wordpress while using Next.js, you can follow these steps:

1. Install the `cors` package in your Next.js app by running this command in your terminal:
   ```bash
   npm install --save cors
   ```

2. Import the `cors` package at the top of your `pages/_app.js` file:
   ```javascript
   import Cors from 'cors';
   ```

3. Create an instance of the `Cors` middleware and apply it to all routes in your `pages/_app.js` file:
   ```javascript
   const cors = new Cors({
     origin: process.env.VERCEL_URL || 'http://localhost:3000', // Replace 'http://localhost:3000' with your localhost URL if you're developing locally
     optionsSuccessStatus: 200, // You can set any HTTP status code here, I prefer 200
   });

   function MyApp({ Component, pageProps }) {
     return (
       <Provider store={store}>
         <Component {...pageProps} />
       </Provider>
     );
   }

   export default cors(MyApp);
   ```

4. In your `pages/api/your-api-route.js` file, add the `cors()` middleware to your API route handler:
   ```javascript
   import { NextResponse } from 'next/server';
   import { fetchData } from './utils';

   const handler = async (req, res) => {
     const data = await fetchData();

     return NextResponse.json(data);
   };

   export const config = {
     api: {
       bodyParser: false, // Disable body parsing since we don't need it
     },
   };

   export default handler;
   ```

After implementing these changes, you should be able to access your Next.js app's API routes from your Wordpress site without encountering any CORS errors. Let me know if you have any further questions!

