Unfortunately, it's not possible for a MATLAB program to accurately determine the tense of a sentence with full grammatical correctness. This is because the English language has complex grammar rules that vary depending on context and speaker. MATLAB is primarily designed for numerical computations and data analysis rather than natural language processing tasks. However, there are some techniques available in MATLAB that can help you identify certain aspects of the tense, such as identifying auxiliary verbs or verb endings. Here's an example MATLAB function that identifies the auxiliary verbs (helps) and modal auxiliaries (would) in a sentence:

```MATLAB
function [aux_verbs, modals] = getAuxiliaryVerbs(sentence)
    % Splits the input sentence into words using spaces as delimiters
    words = strsplit(sentence);

    % Initializes empty arrays to store auxiliary verbs and modals
    aux_verbs = [];
    modals = [];

    % Loops through each word in the sentence
    for I = 1:length(words)

        % Checks if the current word is an auxiliary verb or modal
        switch lower(words(i))
            case {'is', 'are', 'was', 'were', 'be', 'being', 'been'}
                % Adds the auxiliary verb to the auxiliary verbs array
                aux_verbs(end+1, :) = words(i);
            case {'can', 'could', 'may', 'might', 'shall', 'should', 'will', 'would'}
                % Adds the modal to the modals array
                modals(end+1, :) = words(i);
        end
    end
end
```

Using this function, we can call it with our example sentence to see which auxiliary verbs and modals it identifies:

```MATLAB
sentence = "I would like to have a meeting today";
[aux_verbs, modals] = getAuxiliaryVerbs(sentence);
disp(aux_verbs');
disp(modals');
```

