To retrieve the username from the JWT (JSON Web Token) token in ASP.NET C# WebAPI, you can follow these steps:

1. First, make sure that your JWT is properly configured to include the username as a claim. This can be done by adding the username to the payload when generating the token.

2. In your WebAPI controller, install the `JwtBearer` package by running the following command in the Package Manager Console:

   ```powershell
   Install-Package Microsoft.IdentityModel.Tokens -Version 6.4.0
   ```

3. Next, add the following lines to the top of your controller class:

   ```csharp
   using System;
   using System.IdentityModel.Tokens.Jwt;
   using System.Security.Claims;
   using System.Threading.Tasks;
   ```

4. Now, create a method to extract the username from the JWT token. Here's an example implementation:

   ```csharp
   private string GetUsernameFromToken(string token)
   {
       var tokenHandler = new JwtSecurityTokenHandler();
       var tokenDescription = tokenHandler.ReadJwtToken(token);

       if (tokenDescription == null || !tokenDescription.Header.Alg.Equals(SecurityAlgorithms.HmacSha256, StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase))
       {
           throw new SecurityTokenException("Invalid token");
       }

       return tokenDescription.Claims.FirstOrDefault(c => c.Type == ClaimTypes.Name)?.Value;
   }
   ```

5. You can now call this method within your controller actions to retrieve the username from the JWT token. For example:

   ```csharp
