To make the code more efficient, we can follow these steps:

1. Use `useQuery` to fetch all prompts instead of querying them repeatedly in the UI. This will reduce the number of API calls and improve performance.

```jsx
const prompts = api.prompt.all.useQuery();
```

2. Instead of creating multiple mutations for deleting and updating prompts, we can create a single mutation that handles both operations based on the input parameters. This will simplify the code and reduce the number of network requests.

```jsx
const upsertPrompt = api.prompt.upsert.useMutation({
  onSuccess: async () => {
    utils.prompt.invalidate(); // Invalidate cache after successful operation
  },
});

// To update or delete a prompt, pass the appropriate parameters to the mutation
void upsertPrompt.mutateAsync({
  id: selectedTargetPrompt, // If updating, pass the ID of the existing prompt; if deleting, pass a null value
  name: editedName, // If updating, pass the new name of the prompt; if deleting, pass an empty string
  prompt: editedPrompt, // If updating, pass the new prompt text; if deleting, pass an empty string
});
```

3. We can also optimize the UI by using `useState` to manage the selected target prompt, edited name, and edited prompt instead of relying on external functions like `setInputMessage`. This will eliminate unnecessary function calls and improve performance.

```jsx
const [selectedTargetPrompt, setSelectedTargetPrompt] = useState("");
const [editedName, setEditedName] = useState("");
const [editedPrompt, setEditedPrompt] = useState("");

return (
  ...

  <Input
    type="text"
    placeholder="Enter name..."
    value={editedName}
    onChange={(e) => setEditedName(e.target.value)}
    borderRadius="md"
    autoFocus
  />

  <Textarea
    placeholder="Enter prompt..."
    value={editedPrompt}
    onChange={(e) => setEditedPrompt(e.target.value)}

