One possible solution to make the planets less crowded could be to adjust the minimum distance between them during the generation process. Instead of hardcoding the minimum distance at 35 units, you could calculate it dynamically based on the number of generated planets. Here's an updated version of the `generateRandomPlanets` function that does this:

```jsx
const generateRandomPlanets = (numPlanets) => {
  const planets = [];
  let attempts = 0;

  while (planets.length < numPlanets && attempts < 100) {
    attempts++;

    const planet = {
      position: [
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
        randomPosition(-30, 30),
      ],
      size: randomSize(1, 5) * scale,
      color: randomColor(),
      ringSize: 0.2 * scale,
      ringColor: randomColor(),
      ringNoise: 0.3 * scale,
    };

    // Calculate minimum distance based on the number of planets
    const minDistance = 40 - planets.reduce((a, b) => {
      const dist = Math.max(...b.position) - Math.min(...a.position);
      return Math.max(dist, a.ringNoise + a.size / 2 + b.ringNoise + b.size / 2);
    }, Infinity);

    // Check if planet is too close to any existing planet
    const tooClose = planets.some((p) =>
      p.position.some((coord, index) => {
        return Math.abs(coord - planet.position[index]) < minDistance;
      })
    );

    if (!tooClose) {
      planets.push(planet);
    }
  }

  return planets;
}
```

In this version, I first initialize the `minDistance` to `40` units, which is the maximum distance between two planets when they're at opposite ends of the 3D space (-30, 30, -30) and (-30, 30, 30).

