```javascript
const { promisify } = require('util');
const redis = require('redis');

class RedisClient {
  constructor() {
    this.client = new redis();
    this.client.on('error', (err) => console.log('Redis Client Error', err));
  }

  async isAlive() {
    try {
      await this.client.ping();
      return true;
    } catch (err) {
      return false;
    }
  }

  async get(key) {
    return promisify(this.client.get)(key);
  }

  async set(key, value, duration) {
    return promisify(this.client.setex)(key, duration, value);
  }

  async del(key) {
    return promisify(this.client.del)(key);
  }
}

const redisClient = new RedisClient();
module.exports = redisClient;
```

In this implementation, we first import the necessary modules `util` and `redis`. We then define our `RedisClient` class with a constructor that initializes a new Redis client and listens for errors using the `on` method.

The `isAlive` method checks if the Redis client is connected by attempting to ping the server and returning `true` or `false` accordingly.

The `get`, `set`, and `del` methods are all wrapped in Promises using `promisify` from the `util` module, which allows us to await their results in asynchronous code. This makes it easier to handle these operations synchronously and avoid callback hell.

Finally, we create an instance of the `RedisClient` class and export it so it can be easily imported and used elsewhere in our project.

