Three Pivotal Moments During World War I:

1. Battle of Jutland (May 31 - June 1, 1916): This naval battle was fought between the British Royal Navy and the German Kaiserliche Marine in the North Sea. The specific location was off the coast of Denmark. The consequence of this battle was that the British emerged victorious, although both sides suffered significant losses. The lesser-known factor that contributed to the outcome was that the Germans had deciphered some of the British radio communications, allowing them to anticipate certain British maneuvers.

2. Russian Revolution (February-March 1917): Also known as the February Revolution, this event led to the overthrow of Tsar Nicholas II and the establishment of a provisional government. It occurred in Petrograd (now St. Petersburg), Russia's capital at the time. The consequence was that it opened the door for the Bolsheviks (later known as the Communists) to seize power in October of the same year. A lesser-known factor that contributed to the outcome was that food shortages and widespread discontent with the war effort played a major role in sparking the revolution.

3. Armistice (November 11, 1918): This event signaled the end of hostilities between the Allied powers and Germany. It took place near Compiegne, France. The consequence was that it brought about the end of World War I and led to the Treaty of Versailles being signed in 1919. A lesser-known factor that contributed to the outcome was that the United States' entry into the war in April 1917 provided a significant boost to the Allies' resources and morale.

Binary Search Algorithm for a Sorted Array:

