#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

int binarySearch(int arr[], int left, int right, int x) {
    while (left <= right) {
        int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;
        if (arr[mid] == x)
            return mid;
        else if (arr[mid] > x)
            right = mid - 1;
        else
            left = mid + 1;
    }
    return -1;
}

Certainly! Here's an implementation of the binary search algorithm in C++:

```cpp
int binarySearch(int arr[], int left, int right, int x) {
    while (left <= right) {
        int mid = left + (right - left) / 2; // Calculate middle index

        if (arr[mid] == x) { // Element found at middle index
            return mid;
        }
        else if (arr[mid] > x) { // Target element is on the left half
            right = mid - 1;
        }
        else { // Target element is on the right half
            left = mid + 1;
        }
    }

    return -1; // Element not present in array
}
```

This function takes four arguments: `arr` (the sorted array), `left` (the leftmost index of the current subarray being searched), `right` (the rightmost index of the current subarray being searched), and `x` (the value to be searched for).

The basic idea behind binary search is to repeatedly divide the search interval in half until the target element is found or the search interval is empty. In each iteration, we calculate the middle index of the current subarray and check whether the target element is at this index. If it is, we return the index. If it isn't, we update our search bounds based on whether the target element could be in the left or right half of the current subarray. We continue this process until either the target element is found or the search interval becomes empty.

Here's how you might use this function:

