int binarySearch(int arr[], int left, int right, int x) {
    while (left <= right) {
        int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;

        // Check if element is present at middle
        if (arr[mid] == x) {
            return mid;
        }

        // If element is smaller than mid, then it can only be present in left subarray
        if (arr[mid] > x) {
            right = mid - 1;
        }

        // Else element is larger than mid, so it can only be present in right subarray
        else {
            left = mid + 1;
        }
    }

    // Element was not found in array
    return -1;
}
```

This function takes four arguments: the sorted array `arr`, the initial left boundary `left`, the initial right boundary `right`, and the target value `x`. The function returns the index of the target value in the array if it is found, or -1 otherwise.

The algorithm works by repeatedly dividing the search interval in half until the target value is either found or the search boundaries meet in the middle (indicating that the target value is not present). The key to the efficiency of this algorithm is that the search space is cut in half with each iteration, which leads to O(log n) time complexity in the average and worst cases.

One important detail to note is that the array being searched must be sorted in non-decreasing order. If the array is not sorted, the algorithm will not work correctly and may produce incorrect results.

